// The six identifier-case conversions, which are **one native each and one algorithm between
// them**: `snake_case` is the primitive, and `kebab_case`, `upper_snake_case` and
// `upper_kebab_case` are read off it. They were two implementations before 2026-10-30 --
// `meta.snake_case` in Ziman and `pascal_to_snake` in `src/derive.c`, the same conditions written
// twice and agreeing by inspection -- plus a third for kebab inside `derive_properties.zm`
// (`replace(name, {"_": "-"})`).
//
// **`camel_case` is deliberately not `snake_case`'s inverse.** `userID` passes through it
// untouched where a word-splitting version would answer `userId`. That is load-bearing: a
// `#[derive(Json)]` key is `camel_case` over a snake_case field name, and `json.snake_keys`
// documents itself as the inverse of exactly what `camel_case` produces -- a narrower rule, under
// which `userID` becomes `user_i_d` on purpose.
//
// **ASCII, deliberately.** These convert a name; a letter outside ASCII is passed through rather
// than guessed at, which is what the C already did and what settles the disagreement with
// `meta.snake_case`'s Unicode-aware version.
fn show(s: string) {
    printf(
        "{} -> {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {}\n",
        s,
        snake_case(s),
        camel_case(s),
        pascal_case(s),
        kebab_case(s),
        upper_snake_case(s),
        upper_kebab_case(s)
    )
}

fn main() {
    // A capital opens a word after a lower-case letter or a digit.
    show("MiddleName")
    show("middle_name")
    show("line1Address")
    // A *run* of capitals is one word, and splits before the next lower-case letter.
    show("userID")
    show("IDCard")
    // Already in the target shape: unchanged.
    show("idle_timeout_ms")
    show("x")
    // Nothing to convert, and nothing to crash on.
    show("")
    // Not a letter in either case, so passed through by every one of them.
    show("ünïcode_näme")
}
